<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Agent planning · Agentic Bible</title><meta name="description" content="How an agent represents possible steps toward a goal, checks intermediate results, and revises a course of action when assumptions fail."><meta property="og:title" content="Agent planning · Agentic Bible"><meta property="og:description" content="How an agent represents possible steps toward a goal, checks intermediate results, and revises a course of action when assumptions fail."><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Agentic Bible"><meta property="og:url" content="https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-planning/"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary"><meta name="twitter:title" content="Agent planning · Agentic Bible"><meta name="twitter:description" content="How an agent represents possible steps toward a goal, checks intermediate results, and revises a course of action when assumptions fail."><meta name="robots" content="index, follow"><meta name="color-scheme" content="light"><link rel="canonical" href="https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-planning/"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"TechArticle","headline":"Agent planning","description":"How an agent represents possible steps toward a goal, checks intermediate results, and revises a course of action when assumptions fail.","articleSection":"Capabilities","inLanguage":"en","url":"https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-planning/","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-planning/"},"citation":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.04091v3","https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601v2","https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629v3"]}</script><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/Base.DyzAbnuc.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/_id_.w1YKO1jA.css"></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header shell"><a class="wordmark" href="/"><img src="/favicon.svg" width="36" height="36" alt=""><span>Agentic Bible<span class="wordmark-sub">A living reference</span></span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/" aria-current="page">Library</a><a href="/topics/">Topics</a><a href="/about/">About</a><a href="/history/">History</a></nav><a class="header-search" href="/search/" aria-label="Search the library"><svg width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="8.5" cy="8.5" r="5.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"></circle><path d="m13 13 4 4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"></path></svg><span>Search</span><kbd aria-hidden="true">/</kbd></a></header><div class="preview-strip" role="region" aria-label="Edition status"><div class="shell"><span class="status-dot"></span> Preview edition <span class="strip-detail">Sourced working drafts. Editorial review pending.</span></div></div><main id="main" class="shell" tabindex="-1"><div class="article-breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Library</a><span data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>/</span><a href="/topics/capabilities/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Capabilities</a></div><div class="reading-layout" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><article class="article" data-pagefind-body data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><header id="overview" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-pagefind-meta="topic" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Capabilities / Reference entry 09</p><h1 data-pagefind-meta="title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Agent planning</h1><p class="article-deck" data-pagefind-meta="description" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>How an agent represents possible steps toward a goal, checks intermediate results, and revises a course of action when assumptions fail.</p><div class="article-status" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="draft-pill" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="status-dot" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></span> Working draft</span><span data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Human editorial certification pending</span></div><nav class="article-actions" aria-label="Article actions" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="#sources" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources (3)</a><a href="#revision" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision history</a><a href="#share" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Share or cite</a><a href="/contribute/?article=agent-planning" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Contribution options</a></nav></header><aside class="contents" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><details data-article-contents data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><summary data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>On this page</summary><nav aria-label="On this page" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="#overview" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Overview</a><a href="#a-plan-is-a-representation-of-intended-work" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>A plan is a representation of intended work</a><a href="#searching-over-alternatives" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Searching over alternatives</a><a href="#feedback-can-change-the-next-step" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Feedback can change the next step</a><a href="#keep-authority-outside-the-plan" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Keep authority outside the plan</a><a href="#sources" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources & context</a><a href="#revision" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision record</a></nav></details><div class="contents-bottom" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Part of the collection</span><a href="/topics/capabilities/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Capabilities <svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></a></div></aside><div class="prose" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p><strong>Agent planning</strong> is the formation of a proposed course of action toward a goal. A plan can identify intermediate questions or dependencies before the next action is attempted. Plan-and-Solve Prompting provides one research example: it asks a language model to devise a plan and then solve the problem using it. The paper studies prompted reasoning tasks, so its results should not be presented as proof that a deployed <a href="/articles/ai-agent/">agent</a> will execute a plan successfully. <a href="#source-plan-solve">1</a></p>
<h2 id="a-plan-is-a-representation-of-intended-work">A plan is a representation of intended work</h2>
<p>A plan describes work that might happen. An execution record describes work that did happen. For an illustrative reference-library task, “check the cited specification” could be an intended step; the fetched revision and a recorded comparison would be evidence of execution. A model producing a checklist has not thereby checked its items. This distinction is the entry’s operational interpretation, not a measured result from the prompting paper.</p>
<p><a href="/articles/agent-orchestration/">Orchestration</a> concerns how components and work are coordinated. Planning concerns which steps are proposed and why they might lead to the goal. An orchestrator can follow a fixed procedure without generating a new plan; a planner can produce a useful proposal without having permission to execute any of it. The terms answer different questions even when one application combines them.</p>
<h2 id="searching-over-alternatives">Searching over alternatives</h2>
<p>Tree of Thoughts explores a different approach from producing a single uninterrupted answer. It generates intermediate candidate thoughts, evaluates them, and uses search to explore or abandon paths. The paper investigates breadth-first and depth-first approaches on tasks including arithmetic puzzles, writing and crosswords. Its evaluations concern those setups; a model’s self-evaluation of a candidate is not an independent correctness certificate. <a href="#source-tree-thoughts">2</a></p>
<p>In an original example, a planner investigating a terminology change might consider comparing specification versions or locating an announcement. These are alternatives with different evidence requirements. If the announcement contains no date for the actual change, the planner could retain the unresolved question rather than treating the announcement date as the answer. A plausible route would still need source checking.</p>
<p>Search also needs a stopping rule. In this example, the designer could limit the number of candidate routes, source checks and revisions to the plan. The purpose of those proposed limits would be to make incomplete investigation explicit. Exploring more alternatives would consume work without guaranteeing a better answer. This is a design consideration for the example, not a performance comparison of planning methods.</p>
<h2 id="feedback-can-change-the-next-step">Feedback can change the next step</h2>
<p>ReAct interleaves generated reasoning with actions and environmental observations. It offers a research example of choosing subsequent work in light of feedback rather than relying only on an initial answer. Its experiments do not imply that every observation is trustworthy or that the model’s interpretation is correct. <a href="#source-react">3</a></p>
<p>Suppose the library task discovers that the cited revision predates the feature under discussion. A revised plan could seek a later revision and explicitly preserve the earlier mismatch as evidence. By contrast, merely restating the original plan would not address the new information. The example separates a changed assumption from a failed tool request: each could justify different next work.</p>
<h2 id="keep-authority-outside-the-plan">Keep authority outside the plan</h2>
<p>A proposed step such as publishing a correction does not grant <a href="/articles/tool-permissions/">tool permissions</a>. Nor would a source document’s embedded instruction become authoritative because it appeared in a plan. <a href="/articles/prompt-injection/">Prompt injection</a> explains that input boundary. An implementation would need to apply its actual execution policy to each proposed action.</p>
<p>For the example, <a href="/articles/agent-evaluation/">evaluation</a> could check whether the agent revises a contradicted assumption, preserves unresolved dependencies, and stops when evidence is unavailable. <a href="/articles/agent-memory/">Memory</a> could retain a finding for later use, while <a href="/articles/durable-agent-execution/">durable execution</a> could retain which steps completed. Neither should silently turn an intended action into a completed one. Planning is useful when it makes choices inspectable; its value still needs to be tested against the task it is meant to help.</p>
</div><p class="article-feedback" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="/contribute/?article=agent-planning" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Suggest a correction or primary source</a> · Public intake remains on hold; the local synthetic form covers only the original three entries.</p><section class="sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Follow the evidence</p><h2 id="sources-title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources & context</h2><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Primary sources checked on <time datetime="2026-10-17" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>17 October 2026</time>. A source check is not independent editorial approval.</p><ol data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><li id="source-plan-solve" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.04091v3" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Plan-and-Solve Prompting: Improving Zero-Shot Chain-of-Thought Reasoning by Large Language Models <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Wang et al., arXiv</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Version 3, 26 May 2023; ACL 2023. Section 2 separates devising a plan from carrying it out in reasoning prompts; its experiments are not production-agent trials.</p></li><li id="source-tree-thoughts" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601v2" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Tree of Thoughts: Deliberate Problem Solving with Large Language Models <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Yao et al., arXiv</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Version 2, 3 December 2023; NeurIPS 2023. Sections 3–4 describe candidate generation, evaluation and search in selected problem-solving tasks.</p></li><li id="source-react" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629v3" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Yao et al., arXiv</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Version 3, 10 March 2023; section 2 describes reasoning interleaved with actions and observations. Results concern the evaluated environments and models.</p></li></ol></section><section class="revision" id="revision" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><h2 data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision record</h2><dl data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Authorship</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>AI-assisted project draft</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Source check</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><time datetime="2026-10-17" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>17 October 2026</time>. This records source consultation, not editorial certification.</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Human editorial review</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Pending. No human editorial certification is recorded.</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Latest source change</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><time datetime="2026-10-17" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>17 October 2026</time> · Add six sourced entries on agent operating principles · <code data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>54b9d079</code></dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision receipt</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>No separate publication receipt is recorded for this working draft.</dd></div></dl><p class="review-scope" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>The twenty entries received deep reading across external and internal review. Claim verification was sampled; this does not certify every factual claim.</p><a class="text-link" data-source-commit="8a38252384c6fe6024a75b462ad95294a131100c" href="https://github.com/Leshey-Digital/agentic-bible/commits/8a38252384c6fe6024a75b462ad95294a131100c/src/content/articles/agent-planning.md" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>View file history on GitHub <svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></a></section><section class="share-tools" id="share" aria-labelledby="share-title" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Keep a useful reference</p><h2 id="share-title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Share or cite this entry</h2><p id="article-citation" tabindex="0" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>“Agent planning.” Agentic Bible. https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-planning/</p><div class="share-buttons" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><button type="button" data-copy-link hidden data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Copy article link</button><button type="button" data-copy-citation hidden data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Copy citation</button></div><p class="share-status" data-share-status role="status" aria-live="polite" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>The citation and link above remain available to copy manually if clipboard access is un